Lambda is an alternative way to run your backend.

Without Lambda

Your architecture can be:
-----------------------

User
↓
Frontend / Voiceflow
↓
FastAPI
↓
RAG
↓
Qdrant
↓
LLM
↓
FastAPI
↓
Voiceflow / Frontend

====

Here, your FastAPI application is continuously running on a server/cloud platform.

Advantages:

Simple to develop and debug
Works very well for your existing chatbot projects
FastAPI directly handles API requests
Suitable for long-running applications
Easy to test locally

=====


With Lambda

The architecture becomes:

User
↓
Frontend / Voiceflow
↓
API Gateway
↓
AWS Lambda
↓
RAG
↓
Qdrant
↓
LLM
↓
Lambda
↓
API Gateway
↓
Voiceflow / Frontend

====

Here, Lambda runs your backend code when a request comes in.

Advantages:

Serverless — no server to manage
AWS automatically handles execution/scaling
You generally pay based on usage rather than keeping a server running
Good for APIs with intermittent traffic
Fits well into an AWS-native architecture

====

Direct comparison

|                    | Without Lambda              | With Lambda                  |
| ------------------ | --------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Backend            | FastAPI/server              | Lambda function              |
| Server management  | Usually required            | AWS manages it               |
| API Gateway        | Optional                    | Commonly used                |
| Serverless         | ❌                           | ✅                            |
| Your Python code   | Runs continuously/on server | Runs when invoked            |
| Development        | Easier                      | Slightly more setup          |
| Long-running tasks | Better                      | Not ideal                    |
| Scaling            | You configure/manage it     | AWS handles scaling          |
| Best for           | Normal web/API apps         | Event-driven/serverless APIs |

===

For your chatbot projects

If your existing project is:

FastAPI → RAG → Qdrant → LLM

and it already works well, there is no technical need to add Lambda.

====

You would add Lambda mainly if your project requirement is something like:

"Build and deploy the backend using AWS serverless architecture."

So for your diagram, don't add Lambda just because it sounds necessary. If you're using FastAPI, you can show:

Voiceflow → FastAPI → RAG → Qdrant → LLM

If you're specifically demonstrating AWS Serverless, then show:

Voiceflow → API Gateway → Lambda → RAG → Qdrant → LLM.

